  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Institutional Development  CDD</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CPD is a member of the founding members of the Nexus consortium and is currently working in Banaadir State, Galmudug State, Hirshabelle State &lt;(&gt;&amp;&lt;)&gt; South West State. Specifically in Hirshabelle, CPD is implementing projects in Jowhar, Baladweyn, Warsheikh, Cadale and Matabaan districts). CPD works to improve the standard of living and alleviate all deprivations of affected communities by building resilience, providing emergency assistance, mobilizing resources, advocacy, and representing marginalized groups at all levels.

CPD and CRS with support from USAID, is currently implementing the five-year Nabadoon project to transform intercommunal conflict in Somalia&#x2019;s Al Shabab recovered Warsheikh Districts. Nabadoon embraces a People to People (P2P) approach that focuses on cooperation and mutual interest as the most effective pathway to overcome social, economic, and political barriers in the four target districts. Working both horizontally, within and across groups, and vertically, across levels of authority, the project creates a space for citizens and Government of Somalia (GoS) officials to jointly seize the opportunity for peace and development created by the recent liberation from Al Shabab control. The project will bring together clans and sub-clans identified as primary conflict parties. Within these groups, the project promotes inclusion and empowerment of women, youth, pastoralists, coastal communities, as well as historically marginalized communities to reduce intercommunal divisions.</narrative>
  </description>
  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CPD is a Non-Profit, Non-Government Organization (NGO) founded in 2003 and registered in Somalia with the Federal Government and the various Regional Authorities in the areas it operates. The organization has 5 operational offices namely in Mogadishu city, Baidoatown, Galkaio, Hobyo town, Adado town and a liaison office in Nairobi (Kenya). CPD&#x2019;s original mandate was to solely build peace, reduce conflicts, promote human rights, advance good governance and democratization. However, in 2007, the organization expanded its scope to include humanitarian assistance, poverty reduction through socio-economic development for the communities it serves and supporting the country&#x2019;s stabilization initiatives as a measure to respond to the prevailing needs of the communities.
The organization is under the overall management of the Board made up of Directors. The organization&#x2019;s daily management is under the Senior Management Team consisting of 10 members including 9 line Managers and the Executive Director who is in charge of the team. CPD has a diverseand qualified human resource base consisting of 113 staff as of September 2018.
2.0 Vision:
CPD envisions a poverty-free and sustainably-developed Africa.
3.0 Mission:
To facilitate evidenced-based and participatory approaches to development for the sustained welfare of African communities.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Centre for Peace and Democracy</narrative>
